* {{l|en|leucæmia}}, {{l|en|leuchaemia}}, {{l|en|leuchemia}}, {{l|en|leukæmia}}, {{l|en|leuchæmia}}, {{l|en|leukhæmia}} {{qualifier|archaic}}

===Etymology===
From {{bor|en|de|Leukämie}}, from {{der|en|grc|λευκός|t=white}} + {{m|grc|αἷμα|t=blood}}; {{l|en|surface analysis#Noun}}, {{confix|en|leuk|emia}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/luːˈkiːmi.ə/}}
* {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vealhurl-leukemia.wav|Audio (Southern England)}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|~}}

# Any of a class of types of {{l|en|cancer}} affecting the {{l|en|blood cell|blood cells}}, especially the {{l|en|white blood cell|white blood cells}} (usually with massive {{l|en|leukocytosis}}), and the [[blood cell]]–forming ({{l|en|hematopoiesis|hematopoietic}}) tissues.
#: {{ux|en|The subjects suffered from lymphoblastic and myeloid '''leukemias'''.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=2012-01|author=Philip E. Mirowski
|volume=100|issue=1|page=87|magazine={{w|American Scientist}}
|title=[http://www.americanscientist.org/bookshelf/pub/harms-to-health-from-the-pursuit-of-profits Harms to Health from the Pursuit of Profits]
|passage=In an era when political leaders promise deliverance from decline through America’s purported preeminence in scientific research, the news that science is in deep trouble in the United States has been as unwelcome as a diagnosis of '''leukemia''' following the loss of health insurance.}}

